Today I started by improving the overall architecture of the crawler. Instead of directly using the Fetcher and Queue classes throughout the project, I created dedicated wrapper classes around them. The Frontier class now wraps my Queue implementation and is responsible for managing the URLs waiting to be crawled, while the Fetcher wrapper encapsulates all the page downloading functionality. This makes the WebCrawler class cleaner and separates responsibilities between different components.

After that, I continued testing the crawler and noticed a major problem. Although the crawler was visiting many URLs, most of the HTML pages being saved were completely empty. I thought there could be two possible reasons for this. The first possibility was that the fetcher was returning empty pages because requests were being made too quickly. The second possibility was that my URL normalization function was producing incorrect URLs, causing the fetcher to request invalid pages.

I decided to investigate the URL handler first. While reviewing the code, I found a bug in the URL class. The URL components are stored as member variables, and whenever split_url() was called on the same object, the new components were simply appended to the existing ones instead of replacing them. Although this had not caused any visible issues yet, it was clearly incorrect. I fixed it by clearing all the component strings at the beginning of the split_url() function before parsing a new URL.

Next, I moved to the Fetcher class. I realized that some websites redirect HTTP requests to HTTPS using an HTTP 301 Moved Permanently response. By default, libcurl does not automatically follow these redirects, so my fetcher was receiving the redirect response instead of the actual webpage. To fix this, I enabled the CURLOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION option so that libcurl automatically follows redirects.

While improving the fetcher, I also added several useful libcurl options. I set a request timeout so that very slow requests would not block the crawler forever. I added a connection timeout to limit the time spent establishing a connection. I also set a custom User-Agent string instead of using the default libcurl/x.x.x, since some websites may reject requests coming from the default libcurl user agent.

During testing, I discovered another issue in the fetcher. The call to curl_easy_perform() was returning CURLE_OK even when the downloaded HTML string was empty. This meant that the request technically succeeded, but no useful content had been received. I modified the fetcher so that an empty response is treated as a failed fetch instead of a successful one.

After that, I returned to the link extraction logic. I realized that my parser was only handling a limited set of HTML formats. It failed to correctly parse links written with extra spaces such as `<a href="...">`, uppercase tags like `<A HREF="...">`, links without quotation marks such as `<a href=www.example.com>`, and some malformed HTML. Because of these limitations, I rewrote the extraction loop to handle all of these cases more robustly.

While testing the crawler again, I found another bug in the URL resolver. When constructing the resolved URL, I had forgotten to include the port number if one was present in the original URL. I corrected the resolver so that it now preserves the port when required.

I also fixed several bugs in the normalization function related to resolving relative paths containing ../. These fixes made the normalized URLs much more reliable and reduced the number of invalid URLs generated by the crawler.

After improving the crawling pipeline, I started thinking about how to store the downloaded pages. Instead of writing each page directly to disk as soon as it is fetched, I decided it would be better to store them in memory while the crawler is running and save them only after the crawl finishes. This approach should make it easier to switch to different storage backends in the future, such as a database.

To implement this idea, I created a new Page class that stores the information associated with a crawled page, such as its ID, URL, and HTML content. This class acts as the in-memory representation of a page before it is written to permanent storage.

Finally, I started integrating SQLite into the project. I successfully connected the crawler to a SQLite database and implemented the basic functionality to store each page's ID, URL, and HTML content. At this stage, the crawler is able to save downloaded pages into the database successfully, providing the foundation for the storage component of the project.